course: dictionary
lesson: 8011
title: Reek: smell, warning, or smoke
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Reek usually means a strong, unpleasant smell: «The bins reeked of rotten fish in the summer sun.» The clue is rotten fish, which tells you the smell is bad.
explain: Reek can also be a noun, meaning a strong, bad smell. «The reek of old socks filled the changing room.» Here reek comes after the word the, and the sentence is about a smell, so it must be a noun.
explain: Sometimes reek is not about smell at all. «This plan reeks of trouble.» A plan cannot smell, so reek must mean that the plan strongly suggests something bad. Ask yourself: does this make sense as a real smell? If not, look for the figurative meaning.
explain: Watch out for words that sound the same or almost the same. «Rik» means a year, as in «z NOvym rOkom», Happy New Year. It sounds like reek, but it has nothing to do with smells. In Scotland, Grandad says the chimney gives off «a reek», which means smoke. Check the context, and use a dictionary if you are not sure.
fact: Reek as a verb means to have a very strong, unpleasant smell.
fact: A reek, as a noun, is a strong, bad smell.
fact: Reek of means strongly suggesting something bad, as in «This plan reeks of trouble.»
fact: In Scotland and Northern England, reek can mean smoke.
fact: «Rik» sounds like reek but means a year, so do not mix them up.
exercise: «The bins reeked of rotten fish in the summer sun.» What does reeked mean here? | To feel very happy | To be very quiet and still | *To have a very strong, unpleasant smell | To shine brightly in the sun
exercise: «This plan reeks of trouble.» What does reeks of suggest? | It smells of flowers and fresh bread | *It is a very old plan | It is a plan for a picnic in the park | It strongly suggests that something bad is going to happen
exercise: Grandad in Scotland points at the chimney and says, «That reek means someone's got a fire going.» What is the reek? | *Smoke | A happy song | A pile of wet leaves | A bright light
test: «The reek from the drains made the whole street hold their noses.» Which word in this sentence is a noun? | whole | makes | *reek | street
test: In «rik means a year», what does rik mean? | A plan | *A year | A smell | A fire
exam: Which sentence uses reek as a noun? | We reeked after the football match. | *The reek from the bins made us hold our noses. | She could reek of onions after dinner. | They reek of trouble every day.
